  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/d/d2/Journey_Into_Mystery_Vol_1_100.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "(continued from the last issue, where an \"evil\" Thor began to rob the city...)\n\nThe city hurls insults at Thor for tuning against its citizens, and the police even start shooting at him.&nbsp; He puts two and two together and gets \"Hyde.\"&nbsp; He has to get out of the public eye, so he goes back to Don Blake, only to be reminded by Jane that he promised to take her out to dinner for her birthday, at the Ritz Terrace, but Mr. Hyde has overheard the location and crashes their date, holding them both at gunpoint and escorting them to Don's car!\n\nHe orders them to go to his abandoned old castle (<i>where do these abandoned castles keep coming from</i>?)&nbsp; Don is sure that he won't be letting them leave now that he's revealed where he lives!&nbsp; Once there, Hyde ties up Blake and sets a 24-hour time bomb next to him so that if anything happens to him during that time, Don Blake is doomed!&nbsp; Then he takes Jane and leaves, heading for the East River Naval Yard, where he plans to steal a Polaris submarine!\n\nMeanwhile, Don gradually is able to stretch out a hand to touch his cane and is able to strike it, becoming Thor.&nbsp; As Odin watches from Asgard (for two panels anyway), Thor sees the commotion at the yard and finds Hyde entering the sub - they briefly tussle, and Hyde descends, taking Jane with him.&nbsp; Thor easily tears through the hatch and engages Hyde below, but Jane, fearful that Blake will die if something happens to Hyde, covers his dropped hammer with a piece of canvas, delaying Thor from finding it!\n\nThor creates a tornado with his cape, masking his transformation back to Blake, who then searches the area and eventually finds the hammer, all under the cover of the brief whirlwind.&nbsp; Thor rises and is about to strike Hyde, but Jane stops him, pleading to let Hyde escape so that nothing happens to Don Blake.&nbsp; Thor has to let him jump for safety into the depths.\n\nThe police show up, and \"suddenly\" they realize that Hyde was impersonating him (<i>amazing intuition if you ask me - what led them to this conclusion</i>?).&nbsp; However, more importantly, Odin was watching Jane try to thwart Thor's efforts when taking on Hyde, and he's not happy!&nbsp; \"You <i>dared</i> ask me to make <i>that</i> female immortal?\" - \"Petition denied!&nbsp; She is not worthy!\"&nbsp; Thor has no time to explain before Odin disappears.",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "definitely a readable story which had enough good elements to elevate it above reader level.&nbsp; Odin's outrage over seeing Jane trying to prevent Thor from hurting Hyde was understandable, and this little bit of story plot is what ultimately allowed the book to grade higher than it normally would have.&nbsp; Seeing Don and Jane at dinner together, even so briefly, was another positive.&nbsp; They're clearly crazy about each other and even hint at this in their conversation.\n\nThe downsides of the story were the \"abandoned old castle\" troupe, which is getting a little tiresome - where on Earth do these castles keep coming from?&nbsp; This is supposed to be NYC - I can't believe \"castles\" remain abandoned for long!&nbsp; Also, the police having this amazing realization that Thor was being impersonated all because he fought Hyde and even saw Thor letting him get away?&nbsp; Those were negatives that pushed it back below mid-grade.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nOverall, I found this to be a better read than the previous issue.&nbsp; It certainly won't stick in my mind as particularly memorable though, and all the hype at the end of last month about this being the big 100<sup>th</sup> issue didn't seem warranted - this story didn't seem more special than what you'd expect from any <i>other</i> issue.",
  "side_note": [
    "<b>Tales of Asgard:</b>  \"The Storm Giants\" (a story about the boyhood of Thor)\n\nBoy Thor and brother Loki sneak into the castle of the \"thundering Storm Giants.\"&nbsp; They've stolen some of the apples of Iduna.&nbsp; Both boys are tiny in comparison to the giants.&nbsp; Loki pushes Thor into full view of the giants, and a scuffle ensues, with Thor and Loki finally escaping from them.&nbsp; Every time Thor succeeds, he is able to lift Odin's hammer just a little more.&nbsp; Loki was hoping he would get credit.\n\nIt's a sub-par plot, but gives us a little taste of the two as boys.&nbsp; Nothing really notable here."
  ],
  "ownership": "I was lucky enough to get a FN/VF copy of this as part of the 2000 collection, for which I paid a little over $18, finally selling it on eBay in May 2020 for $97.50, netting me almost $68.\n\n[DEVNOTE: Another tape-covered copy in much worse condition, I've elected to keep.]",
